# PRSL-AI — User Feedback & Support

Canonical Version: 1.0
Status: Foundational / Canonical
Primary Domain: Support & Governance
Supporting Domains: Interaction Experience, Accessibility, Privacy, Context, Intelligence

---

## 1. Purpose

User Feedback & Support is the universal user-facing feedback and support environment of the PRSL-AI Ecosystem.

It provides every user with a direct and accessible way to communicate with the system and the organization responsible for its development.

The purpose is not limited to reporting problems.

It exists to allow users to:

- Report problems
- Suggest improvements
- Describe experiences
- Report accessibility concerns
- Report uncomfortable interactions
- Raise privacy concerns
- Request assistance
- Provide positive feedback
- Identify unexpected behavior
- Contribute ideas for future development

Every user is considered a valid source of feedback.

---

## 2. Universal Access

User Feedback & Support is not restricted to:

- Developers
- Beta testers
- Technical users
- Early adopters
- Power users

It is available to all users.

The system must not require technical knowledge to submit feedback.

A user should be able to describe a problem using their own words.

The system may provide optional structured information when useful, but technical terminology must never be a requirement for participation.

---

## 3. Core Principle

The opinion and experience of every user matters.

A person does not need to understand how the system works internally to identify that something is not working correctly for them.

User experience is a legitimate source of product intelligence.

Core principle:

"Every user is a contributor to the evolution of the system."

---

## 4. User-Centered Reporting

The user should be able to communicate naturally.

Examples:

"The iRiel didn't understand what I meant."

"I didn't like the way iRiel answered me."

"The text was too small for me to read."

"I couldn't figure out how to do this."

"This feature would be much better if it worked differently."

"I felt uncomfortable with this interaction."

"The device did something I didn't expect."

These reports should be considered valid even when the user cannot explain the technical cause.

The responsibility for interpreting the underlying technical cause belongs to the appropriate support or engineering process.

---

## 5. Feedback Categories

User Feedback & Support may include:

### Experience

Feedback about the overall experience of using PRSL-AI or iRiel.

### Interaction

Feedback about how iRiel communicates, understands and responds to the user.

### Accessibility

Feedback related to visual, auditory, physical, cognitive or other accessibility requirements.

### Privacy

Concerns about personal data, permissions, memory, context or privacy behavior.

### Support

Requests for assistance with the user's device, system or services.

### Safety

Reports involving potentially unsafe behavior or situations.

### Suggestions

Ideas for improving existing features or creating new capabilities.

### Compliments

Positive experiences and successful interactions are also valid feedback.

### Other

Users may submit information that does not fit an existing category.

The system should never prevent a user from reporting something simply because they do not know which category applies.

---

## 6. Interaction With iRiel

iRiel may help the user create and submit feedback.

For example:

User:
"I didn't like the way you answered me."

iRiel may respond:

"I understand. Do you want to tell me what felt wrong about the response?"

The user may then explain naturally.

iRiel can help structure the report without changing the user's meaning.

The user should remain in control of the final submission.

---

## 7. Feedback Is Not Automatically Agreement

User feedback must be listened to and evaluated.

Listening does not mean that every suggestion will automatically become a product change.

The system may need to:

- Evaluate feasibility
- Evaluate safety
- Evaluate privacy implications
- Evaluate technical constraints
- Compare with broader user needs
- Identify conflicts between requirements
- Prioritize improvements

The user's contribution remains valid even when the requested change is not implemented.

---

## 8. Feedback History

User submissions should remain available as part of the user's support history, subject to applicable privacy and retention policies.

The user should be able to review:

- Submitted feedback
- Date and time
- Category
- Description
- Relevant interactions
- Support responses
- Follow-up communications
- Status
- Resolution information when available

The purpose is transparency and continuity.

The user should not have to repeatedly explain the same situation when the relevant history already exists.

---

## 9. Support Continuity

Support should preserve context whenever possible.

If a user reports an issue and later follows up, the system should be able to associate the follow-up with the original report.

This reduces repetition and unnecessary effort.

The user should not have to become the system's own case manager.

---

## 10. Accountability

User Feedback & Support also provides an accountability mechanism.

If a user believes they were:

- Disrespected
- Dismissed
- Misunderstood
- Treated unfairly
- Given an inappropriate response
- Made uncomfortable
- Subjected to an accessibility failure

they should be able to preserve and reference the relevant interaction.

The support system should allow the user to request review when appropriate.

Feedback therefore serves two purposes:

1. Improving the system
2. Protecting the user's right to be heard

---

## 11. Privacy

Feedback may contain personal or sensitive information.

The system must therefore apply appropriate privacy controls.

Feedback should not automatically become part of the user's long-term AI memory.

Feedback storage, processing and retention must be governed separately.

The user should understand when information is being:

- Stored
- Shared
- Used for support
- Used for product improvement
- Used for diagnostics
- Retained for accountability

Privacy must remain a fundamental part of the feedback experience.

---

## 12. Accessibility

User Feedback & Support must itself be accessible.

Users should be able to submit feedback through appropriate interaction methods, including where supported:

- Voice
- Text
- Assistive technologies
- Alternative input methods
- Accessible visual interfaces
- Simplified interaction flows

A user should never be unable to report an accessibility problem because the feedback system itself is inaccessible.

---

## 13. Feedback and Product Evolution

User Feedback & Support creates a continuous feedback loop between real-world use and product evolution.

The conceptual cycle is:

User Experience
↓
Feedback
↓
Support / Analysis
↓
Evaluation
↓
Improvement
↓
Updated Experience
↓
New Feedback

This creates a living relationship between the system and the people who use it.

---

## 14. Universal Feedback vs Technical Feedback

User Feedback & Support is intentionally separated from Technical Feedback.

### User Feedback & Support

Focus:

"How does the system affect the user?"

Audience:

All users.

Examples:

- "The response was confusing."
- "I couldn't use this feature."
- "The interface is difficult to read."
- "I felt uncomfortable with this interaction."

### Technical Feedback

Focus:

"How does the system behave technically?"

Audience:

Developers, beta testers and technical participants.

Examples:

- Crash reports
- Bugs
- Regressions
- Performance problems
- API issues
- SDK issues
- Hardware behavior
- Logs
- Integration failures

The two channels may interact operationally, but they remain distinct areas of specialization.

---

## 15. No Technical Barrier

Users should never be required to diagnose a technical problem before reporting it.

The user reports what happened.

The system and support organization determine why it happened.

For example:

User:

"The iRiel stopped remembering what we were talking about."

The user does not need to know whether the cause was:

- Context recovery
- Memory storage
- Session state
- Model routing
- Synchronization
- Hardware
- Software

The technical investigation happens after the report.

---

## 16. Human-Centered Support

Support should communicate in a way that respects the user's preferred interaction style.

The same principles established by Interaction Experience apply to support:

- Natural communication
- Respect
- Accessibility
- Context awareness
- Personalization
- Non-judgment
- Clear explanations
- Minimal unnecessary bureaucracy

The user should not feel that they are entering a hostile technical system simply because they need assistance.

---

## 17. Escalation

A feedback or support case may be escalated when necessary.

Potential escalation paths include:

- General support
- Accessibility specialists
- Privacy specialists
- Security specialists
- Product teams
- Engineering teams
- Governance
- Human review

The user should not need to understand the organization's internal structure.

The system should route the case to the appropriate specialty.

---

## 18. Transparency

When appropriate, users should be able to understand what happened to their feedback.

Possible states include:

- Received
- Under review
- Investigating
- Waiting for information
- Resolved
- Implemented
- Declined
- Archived

A declined suggestion should not disappear without explanation when an explanation is appropriate.

Transparency helps establish trust.

---

## 19. Feedback as Product Intelligence

User Feedback & Support is not merely a customer-service channel.

It is also a source of product intelligence.

Patterns across feedback can reveal:

- Usability problems
- Accessibility gaps
- Unexpected behaviors
- Cultural misunderstandings
- Interaction problems
- Privacy concerns
- Missing capabilities
- New user needs

The system should be capable of identifying patterns while respecting privacy and governance requirements.

---

## 20. User Ownership

The user owns their experience and has the right to communicate concerns about it.

The system must not discourage legitimate feedback simply because it is critical.

Negative feedback is not system failure by definition.

It is information.

The purpose of feedback is to discover where reality differs from intention.

---

## 21. Relationship With Interaction Experience

Interaction Experience defines how iRiel interacts with the human.

User Feedback & Support provides a formal channel through which the human can respond to that experience.

Interaction Experience:

"The machine adapts to the human."

User Feedback & Support:

"The human can tell the machine how well it is adapting."

Together they establish a continuous human-system relationship.

---

## 22. Core Principles

### Universal

Every user can participate.

### Accessible

Every user should be able to provide feedback.

### Human-Centered

Users communicate in their own language.

### Non-Technical

Technical knowledge is not required.

### Transparent

Users can understand the status of their reports.

### Accountable

Users can challenge inappropriate interactions.

### Private

Feedback is handled according to privacy and retention policies.

### Continuous

Feedback contributes to continuous improvement.

### Respectful

Every user deserves to be heard.

---

## 23. Canonical Statement

User Feedback & Support exists because the people who use PRSL-AI are not merely recipients of technology.

They are participants in its evolution.

Every experience matters.

Every voice matters.

Every accessibility need matters.

Every concern deserves a place to be heard.

The system should not ask the user to understand the machine before the machine listens to the user.

---

## 24. Canonical Status

User Feedback & Support is established as a foundational PRSL-AI concept in Canonical Version 1.0.

This version establishes the universal feedback and support philosophy, accessibility requirements, privacy boundaries, accountability model, feedback history and separation from Technical Feedback.

Future versions may refine:

- Support workflows
- Feedback categories
- Escalation mechanisms
- Analytics
- Privacy controls
- Accessibility
- User interfaces
- AI-assisted feedback processing
- Human review processes

Future implementation changes must preserve the foundational principle:

"Every user is a contributor to the evolution of the system."